odes":[{"id":1,"type":0,"content":"You cannot overwrite the official `@neutralinojs/neu` npm package since you don't have publishing rights to that specific package name. However, you have several options to distribute your enhanced version:

## Option 1: Publish under a different scoped name
<augment_code_snippet path="package.json" mode="EDIT">
```json
{
 "name": "@yourusername/neu",
 "version": "11.5.0",
```
</augment_code_snippet>

## Option 2: Publish under a different unscoped name
<augment_code_snippet path="package.json" mode="EDIT">
```json
{
 "name": "neu-enhanced",
 "version": "11.5.0",
```
</augment_code_snippet>

## Option 3: Fork and distribute via GitHub
Keep the same name but install directly from your GitHub repository:
```bash
npm install -g username/neutralinojs-cli
```

## Recommendation
Based on your codebase, I can see you've already implemented custom repository support with `--owner` and `--branch` options in commands like `update` and `create`. This is indeed a valuable enhancement.

I'd suggest Option 1 with your own scope, as it:
- Clearly indicates it's your enhanced version
- Allows easy installation: `npm install -g @yourusername/neu`
- Maintains compatibility with existing workflows
- Gives you full control over releases

Users can then install your version instead of the official one, and all the `neu` commands will work the same way but with your custom repository features.
