},{"status":"success","request_id":"993c8a7e-9f34-4752-9913-24e482ea5f78","request_message":"How do I publish it to npm?","rich_text_json_repr":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I publish it to npm?"}]}]},"mentioned_items":[],"structured_output_nodes":[{"id":1,"type":0,"content":"Here's how to publish your enhanced version to npm:

## 1. Create an npm account
```bash
npm adduser
# or if you already have an account:
npm login
```

## 2. Update package.json with your scope
<augment_code_snippet path="package.json" mode="EDIT">
```json
{
 "name": "@yourusername/neu",
 "version": "11.5.0"
}
```
</augment_code_snippet>

## 3. Ensure you have the right files
Your `package.json` already has the correct `bin` configuration:
```json
"bin": {
 "neu": "./bin/neu.js"
}
```

## 4. Test locally first
```bash
# Install dependencies
npm ci

# Test the package works
npm link
neu --help
```

## 5. Publish to npm
```bash
# For scoped packages, you need to specify public access
npm publish --access=public
```

## 6. Users can then install your version
```bash
npm install -g @yourusername/neu
```

## Important notes:
- Replace `@yourusername` with your actual npm username/organization
- Your package name must be unique on npm
- The `--access=public` flag is required for scoped packages to be publicly installable
- Consider updating the `repository.url` in `package.json` to point to your fork
- You might want to update the `homepage` and `bugs.url` fields too

Your enhanced version with custom `--owner` and `--branch` support will then be available for others to use!
